WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.356
- Talent is the great equalizer

00:00:04.356 --> 00:00:06.406
of all the things 
that people are scared of.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.942
Don't worry America 
everything will be fine.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.210
Talent still matters.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.579
(upbeat music)

00:00:24.825 --> 00:00:26.159
- You were born 
in the Soviet Union.

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:26.827
- [Gary] I was.

00:00:26.827 --> 00:00:27.751
- [Rebecca] How much 
do you think that shaped

00:00:27.751 --> 00:00:29.863
who you are now?
- An enormous amount.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.933
I think being born in a 
Communist country as a purebred

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:36.537
capitalist and then having the
great fortune to be able to come

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:39.773
to America and then to 
be really not wealthy and

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:40.941
not have stuff as a kid.

00:00:40.941 --> 00:00:42.242
To be emotional that my parents

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.444
bought me two "Star Wars"
figures when I was six and

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:47.881
knowing that was 
an enormous deal.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:51.418
To know that when I wanted stuff
my mom was very quick to tell me

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:55.031
go buy it yourself 
and go earn it.

00:00:55.031 --> 00:00:58.859
It absolutely, absolutely, 
absolutely shaped me.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.194
And the other thing, you know, 
especially in the political

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:04.364
climate we all live in now, 
I don't think people understand

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:05.699
how amazing America is.

00:01:05.699 --> 00:01:07.501
It makes me 
enormously optimistic,

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:11.371
very positive and those are very
important traits if you're gonna

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:14.274
be insane and actually 
go after entrepreneurship.

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:15.609
- How did you do 
this whole thing?

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:17.077
You built it from the ground up.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.346
- I've been producing 
content online for 10 years.

00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:22.516
Early YouTube when I started the
wine show for my prior career.

00:01:22.516 --> 00:01:25.098
(upbeat jazz music)

00:01:27.955 --> 00:01:29.523
And about two,
three years ago,

00:01:29.523 --> 00:01:31.325
I wasn't doing 
the wine show any more.

00:01:31.325 --> 00:01:33.860
I was once in a blue 
moon putting out some content

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.028
but it was building up.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:36.430
I'm like there's an 
opportunity for me to talk.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.459
There's new thoughts 
in my mind and we started

00:01:38.459 --> 00:01:40.334
a show called 
The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:43.042
You ask questions,

00:01:43.042 --> 00:01:45.752
and I answer them.

00:01:45.752 --> 00:01:51.111
This is The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:55.482
And then vlogging started taking
off on YouTube more and more and

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:58.719
so kind of the marriage of 
YouTube first person vlogging

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:02.589
and reality TV became the 
concept behind DailyVee which we

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:06.059
started earlier this year and
it's become a very substantial

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:09.295
part of the brand building 
of myself and my agency.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:12.898
("Legendary" by Welshly Arms)

00:02:16.578 --> 00:02:18.346
- [Gary] I think it's really
smart to create a

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:20.007
North Star and reverse engineer.

00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:23.176
Seven years ago, after making
a lot of money on investments,

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.277
decided to build an agency.

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.279
Full service, 
media buying, creative,

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.849
strategy, 
video production, all of it.

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:31.351
Not just one like the rest of
the agency world and I was gonna

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.519
build it for myself.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:35.088
And so I was 
gonna take a step back,

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.791
eat crap 'cause you have to 
have clients and that stinks.

00:02:37.791 --> 00:02:39.359
Especially when you've 
already made your money.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:40.560
- How do you do that?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.396
'Cause you're 
very outspoken person.

00:02:42.396 --> 00:02:44.364
- [Gary] I do it 
'cause I eat crap.

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:45.499
'Cause they're the boss.

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:47.034
My dad has a Russian saying:

00:02:47.034 --> 00:02:49.836
whoever pays for the 
music gets to pick it.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.204
- When you were 
in those first years,

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.407
you took advantage of YouTube 
very early on as you said.

00:02:53.407 --> 00:02:54.074
- Yes.

00:02:54.074 --> 00:02:56.443
- Was there ever a fear of well
if I'm not creating the video

00:02:56.443 --> 00:02:59.813
content maybe I'm 
focusing on the wrong thing.

00:02:59.813 --> 00:03:02.949
- I've only had one substantial
advantage against the market

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:05.619
which is I just 
worked 13 and 14

00:03:05.619 --> 00:03:06.520
and 15 hours a day.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.389
If you're competing against 
people that are working seven

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:13.060
and eight and really five and 
six when he count in lunch and a

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.896
couple of YouTube 
videos and a phone call or two,

00:03:15.896 --> 00:03:18.031
I was never worried about that.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:21.568
I always had enough time 
to be what I am which is the

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:24.704
CEO of a $100 million 
dollar revenue agency and,

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.873
you know especially with the
infrastructure around now 'cause

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.709
I'm a five person team which

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:30.710
really a modern-day
production company.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.380
- What's been the hardest lesson
for you to learn in your life?

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.015
- [Gary] That 
I can't do everything.

00:03:35.015 --> 00:03:35.916
- What are things you would

00:03:35.916 --> 00:03:37.751
love to be doing 
that you're not doing?

00:03:37.751 --> 00:03:40.353
- Nothing. I'm in 
full control my life.

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:41.855
If there's 
something I want to do,

00:03:41.855 --> 00:03:42.956
then I can go do it.

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:45.358
We make our beds 
so I sleep in it.

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:47.260
- [Rebecca] What do you think
about all the evangelists today

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.229
who talk about 
you need more sleep,

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:50.163
you need to work less?

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:52.766
- All of it is very personal.

00:03:52.766 --> 00:03:56.069
I don't need society telling
me how to do work-life balance.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.973
I don't need society telling 
me how to raise my children.

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:02.742
That being said, I'm a 
huge advocate of sleep.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.277
I'm all-in for six, seven.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:07.180
I mean I always try to get 
six or seven hours of sleep.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.615
That's plenty.

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:11.318
I'm just more worried about what
you're doing while you're awake.

00:04:11.318 --> 00:04:13.205
- Right. Not wasting your time.

00:04:13.205 --> 00:04:14.627
- [Gary] 100%.

00:04:14.627 --> 00:04:15.322
- One of your main things--

00:04:15.322 --> 00:04:17.190
- If you're complaining, 
now by the way,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.892
this is only if you complain.

00:04:18.892 --> 00:04:21.194
If you don't complain, 
you can do anything you want.

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:24.033
This is for the people saying, 
"I wish I had," or

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:27.032
"You got lucky," or 
"This is unfair,"

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:28.752
for those people if you're on

00:04:28.752 --> 00:04:30.424
four softball teams and watch

00:04:30.424 --> 00:04:35.275
entire seasons of stuff on 
Netflix, you can't complain.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:37.210
- Do you watch Netflix?
- I don't.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:41.333
I consume the reactions 
of my community

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:44.393
to my content and
that's why I over index

00:04:44.393 --> 00:04:46.019
with my community 
'cause I've allocated

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:49.422
my leisure time or my escapism--
- To reading your comments?

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:53.654
- and providing value and I hope
through example and content

00:04:53.654 --> 00:04:58.031
I'm able to inspire that but 
I think practicality is grossly

00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:02.002
underrated and 
for all my sizzle,

00:05:02.002 --> 00:05:03.470
I'm obsessed with the steak.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.807
- Because in the world of advice
giving there is tons of sizzle

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.209
and not a lot of steak.

00:05:09.209 --> 00:05:10.377
Does that frustrate you?

00:05:10.377 --> 00:05:12.612
- I don't think I'm in the 
advice giving business and

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.447
I understand why 
everybody would think that.

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:17.250
I think I'm in the documenting 
what I'm up to business.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.585
A quick little 
walk to the Garden.

00:05:18.585 --> 00:05:21.387
Haven't done this yet 
so I'm excited about that.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.492
Oh, DRock, look at that.

00:05:22.492 --> 00:05:24.224
They're doing the, what is that?

00:05:24.224 --> 00:05:25.725
- [DRock] What 
are you pointing at?

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:27.060
- [Rebecca] What do 
you think it is then

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.862
that attract people
to you?

00:05:28.862 --> 00:05:31.731
- [Gary] I think they understand
genuinely that I have no

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:34.501
interest in getting 
anything out of them.

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:38.171
The only thing I want from them
is their attention and in return

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.773
I'm willing to give 
them everything I got.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:45.344
Oh!

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:47.714
I think when 
I look at my contemporaries

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:50.917
AKA other people that are 
in the advice giving business,

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:53.486
there's always a 
punchline at the end.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:56.423
Sign up for my course, 
come to my event,

00:05:56.423 --> 00:05:58.792
travel to my destination thing.

00:05:58.792 --> 00:06:00.594
I don't want anything from them.

00:06:00.594 --> 00:06:05.262
I just want the thing that 
my mom gave so much to me

00:06:05.262 --> 00:06:07.703
that I still need it which 
is I want their attention.

00:06:07.703 --> 00:06:10.995
- What do you think of the 
influencers and the desire among

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:13.406
so many people to 
become influencers now?

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:14.471
- I'll separate them.

00:06:14.471 --> 00:06:16.628
What do I think about 
the actual influencers?

00:06:16.628 --> 00:06:17.678
That they're the one of the

00:06:17.678 --> 00:06:21.181
two or three best 
deals in advertising.

00:06:21.181 --> 00:06:23.750
What do I think of everybody's 
aspiration to be one?

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.085
Makes a whole lot of sense.

00:06:25.085 --> 00:06:28.021
Sounds like a lot of fun to make
$300,000 a year taking selfies

00:06:28.021 --> 00:06:30.928
with products on Instagram.
- [Rebecca] But?

00:06:30.928 --> 00:06:33.794
- But 99% of the people 
watching aren't talented enough

00:06:33.794 --> 00:06:35.061
to actually create an audience.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:36.630
- What do you 
think because, you know,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.101
everything about who you are--

00:06:39.101 --> 00:06:41.237
- By the way, 
everybody should try.

00:06:41.237 --> 00:06:42.764
- Really?
- Of course.

00:06:42.764 --> 00:06:45.404
Let me rephrase everybody 
who wants to should try.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.234
- [Rebecca] This is why I think,

00:06:47.234 --> 00:06:49.042
this is why I question 
your thoughts on that.

00:06:49.042 --> 00:06:50.143
- Go ahead.
- Because every--

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:51.811
- I'm excited this 
is where it gets fun.

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:52.713
Go ahead.

00:06:52.713 --> 00:06:56.750
- Everything you stand for 
authenticity and being true to

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.852
what you are, who you are.
- [Gary] Yes.

00:06:58.852 --> 00:07:01.154
- There are so many 
influencers now who,

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:04.157
not all them but a lot of 
them, in order to make that

00:07:04.157 --> 00:07:07.494
$300,000 a year you have 
to endorse tons of products,

00:07:07.494 --> 00:07:10.130
some of which you probably 
don't actually stand for.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.700
- Yeah and so what will happen
over time is that audience will

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:16.436
start to walk away from that 
person if they're not creating

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:17.904
enough value in return.

00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:21.107
I think it's a value exchange.

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:24.477
I also think that we're 
educating our clients of just

00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:27.914
'cause she has 4 million 
followers if when she says buy

00:07:27.914 --> 00:07:30.684
this, if they don't believe her
you're gonna waste money and

00:07:30.684 --> 00:07:33.286
I think as the maturity of 
the market understands that

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:35.989
authentic execution's 
gonna really matter.

00:07:35.989 --> 00:07:37.457
- That's what you 
built your business on.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:40.026
- [Gary] Listen, I have never 
done a sponsorship deal on my

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:43.217
content in 10 years and 
I got offered in 2006 to put

00:07:43.217 --> 00:07:45.392
bottled water on the 
table of Wine Library TV.

00:07:45.392 --> 00:07:46.399
- Why didn't you do it then?

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:48.268
- Because I knew I was gonna 
make money in a different way

00:07:48.268 --> 00:07:50.033
and I didn't want 
to lose the exchange.

00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:53.239
Once you ask for something, 
you've lost leverage.

00:07:53.239 --> 00:07:55.542
- What was the lowest point 
for you so far along the way?

00:07:55.542 --> 00:07:58.812
- Fourth through 12th grade.
- Why?

00:07:58.812 --> 00:08:02.482
- Because I wasn't built to 
go through that horse crap of a

00:08:02.482 --> 00:08:04.150
education machine that America

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.720
is for kids that are 
purebred entrepreneurs.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.255
- What were some of 
the experiences you had?

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:11.324
- Teachers telling me that 
I would never succeed in life

00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:14.127
while I was making $3,000 a 
weekend selling baseball cards

00:08:14.127 --> 00:08:15.695
and making more money 
than they were making.

00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:19.132
For all my bravado and 
ego, I got really lucky.

00:08:19.132 --> 00:08:20.934
My parents gave 
me the right DNA.

00:08:20.934 --> 00:08:21.968
I have a lot of humility.

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:24.170
I didn't need to 
prove it to those teachers.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.105
I didn't need to razz them.

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:28.775
I didn't need to stick it to my
friends' parents who didn't want

00:08:28.775 --> 00:08:31.077
their kids hanging out with 
me because I'd be a bad school

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:33.046
influence and I wasn't 
gonna be a winner.

00:08:33.046 --> 00:08:35.315
I grew up in the era 
in the '80s and '90s when

00:08:35.315 --> 00:08:37.683
education was 
the only way to win.

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:38.784
You were gonna be successful

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:40.720
if you went to 
Harvard and then won.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.390
There was no conversation 
around entrepreneurship.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:45.825
When I was a kid, entrepreneur 
meant that you were a loser.

00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:48.061
- What is the worst advice 
you've received along the way?

00:08:48.061 --> 00:08:50.163
- Almost everything 
anybody's ever told me.

00:08:50.163 --> 00:08:52.732
Advice is bad 
unless it's contextual and

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:53.833
you really dig deep.

00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:57.103
I give very little advice to 
my employees until I start

00:08:57.103 --> 00:08:58.304
to really get to know them.

00:08:58.304 --> 00:09:01.574
And so, most people like to 
give advice without context.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:04.978
The best advice only comes from
the people closest to you and

00:09:04.978 --> 00:09:06.479
the ones that actually have

00:09:06.479 --> 00:09:09.182
your best interest 
in mind, not theirs.

00:09:09.182 --> 00:09:11.818
That means almost nobody because
that means everybody's on the

00:09:11.818 --> 00:09:14.587
outside and a lot of 
times the people on the inside.

00:09:14.587 --> 00:09:15.855
I mean mommy and daddy are

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:18.825
giving you advice in 
their best interest, not yours.

00:09:18.825 --> 00:09:21.261
- So how do you differentiate?

00:09:21.261 --> 00:09:22.595
- I just don't 
listen to anybody.

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:24.731
- Has that come back to hurt 
you in any way do you think?

00:09:24.731 --> 00:09:27.600
- I'm sure but 
I'll take where I'm at.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.237
And I mean in 
life, not in business.

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:34.307
It's so funny how your flaws can
end up becoming your strengths.

00:09:34.307 --> 00:09:38.745
My enormous need for attention
has made me give so much more to

00:09:38.745 --> 00:09:40.447
my audience than I ask in return

00:09:40.447 --> 00:09:42.315
because I know that 
will give more attention.

00:09:42.315 --> 00:09:47.213
My sense of self has made me
obsess over how many people are

00:09:47.213 --> 00:09:50.790
gonna show up to my funeral 
which then makes me want to be

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.194
nice to everybody
along the way because--

00:09:54.194 --> 00:09:55.301
- [Rebecca] What do 
you want them to

00:09:55.301 --> 00:09:56.596
say about you at your funeral?

00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:57.263
- [Gary] That he was the

00:09:57.263 --> 00:09:58.798
greatest human 
being that ever lived.

00:09:58.798 --> 00:10:00.400
- [Rebecca] The greatest 
human being that ever lived?

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.202
- [Gary] Yep.
- That didn't take my advice.

00:10:02.202 --> 00:10:04.604
- Yeah, I mean I don't 
think anybody's gonna be there,

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:07.207
like at my funeral nobody's 
gonna be there that ever tried

00:10:07.207 --> 00:10:08.808
to give me advice.

00:10:08.808 --> 00:10:11.511
Because there's only, 
there's nobody left.

00:10:11.511 --> 00:10:15.148
It's unbelievable how 
visceral I am to advice.

00:10:15.148 --> 00:10:16.649
Nobody even attempts it anymore.

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:19.552
- For people out 
there who are feeling stuck,

00:10:19.552 --> 00:10:20.820
they've been trying over and

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.288
over again to 
get what they want.

00:10:22.288 --> 00:10:23.990
It's not working out, 
what do they do?

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:28.761
- Something severe like get a
divorce or stop talking to your

00:10:28.761 --> 00:10:31.030
dad or quit your job.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:32.031
I mean it.
I mean it.

00:10:32.031 --> 00:10:34.167
Let me tell you why, stop 
talking to your dad sounds like

00:10:34.167 --> 00:10:36.736
terrible advice until you 
start realizing the thing that

00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:39.239
I've come across over the last
year or two which is most people

00:10:39.239 --> 00:10:40.740
don't do things because they're

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.475
scared to fail in 
front of somebody.

00:10:42.475 --> 00:10:45.144
And then you start unwinding 
and you realize my God they're

00:10:45.144 --> 00:10:47.914
scared to fail in front of 
somebody who's within their

00:10:47.914 --> 00:10:50.250
inner circle, 
who they love the most,

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.685
who's holding them down.

00:10:52.685 --> 00:10:55.021
Poison is bad.
It's bad.

00:10:55.021 --> 00:10:57.056
- When is the last 
time you were afraid?

00:10:57.056 --> 00:10:58.525
- I'm afraid everyday.

00:10:58.525 --> 00:10:59.626
- What are you afraid of?

00:10:59.626 --> 00:11:01.995
- The health and
well-being of my family.

00:11:01.995 --> 00:11:03.663
And I mean for real.

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:06.499
I'm actually more scared 
than almost anybody I know.

00:11:06.499 --> 00:11:07.867
I've never been 
scared in business.

00:11:07.867 --> 00:11:09.302
- You're neurotic?

00:11:09.302 --> 00:11:11.437
- [Gary] I'm just genuinely 
concerned about the health and

00:11:11.437 --> 00:11:12.472
well-being of my family.

00:11:12.472 --> 00:11:15.041
- What are you afraid of?
- Them dying.

00:11:15.041 --> 00:11:16.776
I love them very, very much.

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:18.111
I'm being very serious with you.

00:11:18.111 --> 00:11:20.141
Work? Never.

00:11:20.141 --> 00:11:22.649
- Usually I ask people for their
best piece of advice to people

00:11:22.649 --> 00:11:25.107
but since you're not in the
advice giving business--

00:11:25.107 --> 00:11:25.832
- Yes.

00:11:25.832 --> 00:11:29.088
- for people who see what you've
built and they wish to emulate

00:11:29.088 --> 00:11:33.026
it in some way, what do they do?

00:11:33.026 --> 00:11:38.498
- They have to punt almost
everything besides their family

00:11:38.498 --> 00:11:42.802
and work 24/7/365 hard as hell.

00:11:42.802 --> 00:11:46.011
(house music)

